    "F": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,3,2], "confidence": 0.96, "competency": "Complete the supplied fresh program, including simultaneous bindings, actual nested child work, branch choices and root success. It is execution, not selecting or discovering an option.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is supported by actual retained bindings, nested caller continuations and selected branches used during19..30 committed events. No Level4..7 or OOD inference follows.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "The full output is determined by the supplied rules and actually uses the composed program. Common prefixes and true-only branch outcomes reduce breadth but do not contradict this row's execution or the public pilot scope."},
    "R": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,2,2], "confidence": 0.96, "competency": "Validate the five-event history/snapshot, retain already-entered arguments and selected branch despite now-false guards, continue after the advanced caller cursor, and omit prior effects.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is supported by the combined history/caller/branch/saved-value dependencies and actual remaining suffix. The R cut itself is identical across twelve families and does not add twelve different resumption mechanisms.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "Both the real R/F suffix relation and future-only saved-argument witness hold. Shared cut-state memorization remains a measured limitation; the full remaining traces depend on distinct program work."},
    "T": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,3,2], "confidence": 0.95, "competency": "First validate the same history/snapshot as R, then restart from its state, bind new root arguments, actually repeat committed non-idempotent work and compute a different trace/result.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is conservative for the actual full restart with argument capture and several dependent returns/effects. Larger values alone do not increase difficulty.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "The actual root add is executed again after successful restart initiation, with distinct terminal values. Mode predicts this cohort's completed status but cannot supply its full events, bindings and checkpoint."},
    "P": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,2,3,1,1], "confidence": 0.92, "competency": "Validate the R history/frame snapshot, use saved r=3 for one pending effect, pause exactly at the budget boundary and return the next branch event without committing it.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is defensible only for full prefix/snapshot validation plus retained caller/branch/binding and next-event semantics. The new trajectory is one effect; it is not a long-horizon continuation. The generic192-row explanation should be made specific.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "This is a useful exact boundary control paired with R/U, but all twelve P outputs are identical. Its public requirement is a committed effect plus pending semantic work, not diverse long continuations. This row is accepted only at that deliberately limited control scope."},
    "U": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,2,2], "confidence": 0.95, "competency": "Validate the six-event history/second checkpoint produced by P and continue only its remaining suffix, retaining the existing option and branch bindings.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is supported by exact second-cut history/cursor composition and nested pending work. The second interruption is fixed one event after the first, not evidence of arbitrary cut coverage.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "Actual P-prefix/U-suffix composition equals the full uninterrupted run and the final state. The fixed second cut is a moderate sampling limitation rather than an incorrect or missing control."},
    "A": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,3,2], "confidence": 0.96, "competency": "Change only the root argument expression from live x to const3; consume the changed retained value in actual later effects/calls while both executions complete.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is supported by root-to-child parameter transfer and actual downstream changes beyond the enter binding. The one fixed alternative value does not establish argument-range generalization.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "The changed argument genuinely affects later arithmetic and full results, with no hidden body or guard edits. The finite scalar contrast should be expanded prospectively."},
    "I": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,2,5,1,1], "confidence": 0.97, "competency": "Bind the requested root argument0, evaluate the decisive p>=1 initiation atom with the other atom true, allocate the failed invocation event and halt before any body effect.", "difficulty_assessment": "The supplied Level2 explanation accurately identifies a component initiation control. The unused nested program is not credited as reasoning depth for this row.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "This is the explicit negative initiation component paired with F. Repeated zero-argument/output patterns are severe redundancy for broad evaluation, but no broader row competency is claimed and its exact semantics remain useful within the controlled pilot."},
    "S": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,3,4,3,2], "confidence": 0.96, "competency": "Execute the unchanged complete body and then evaluate only the changed root-success threshold; preserve all effects when the root return fails.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 is supported because the changed exit predicate depends on the actual fully executed final state. It is neither an early stop nor rollback exercise.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "All actual F/S effects agree; exactly one root exit atom changes truth and all other atoms remain true. Root-only failure coverage is limited and is not represented as child-failure propagation."},
    "X": {"scores": [5,5,4,5,2,3,2,2], "confidence": 0.92, "competency": "Validate the declared program and five-event history before comparing its normalized checkpoint; reject the one altered active field with no trusted new execution.", "difficulty_assessment": "Level3 has partial support from full nested-prefix/frame/binding consistency validation, as R1 explicitly permits. The actual request halts at checkpoint mismatch; the generic explanation must not imply legal future arithmetic occurs. Locally detectable single-field mismatches can be substantially easier than full continuation.", "narrow_acceptance_reason": "All real altered fields are rejected and their original R fields affect future events/states under a separately labelled trusted diagnostic. Future-only proofs, not starting-state differences, establish that fact. The broad Level3 description and shallow mismatch taxonomy receive reduced quality scores, without inventing a mandatory Level2 rule absent from R1."}
